    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-c/q2",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q2-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The table below shows the number of revolutions of a drill bit N , and the maximum temperature T C ° reached by this drill bit after revolving for one minute. N 600 700 900 1050 1300 T 49 47 52 52 53 a) State, with a reason, which is the explanatory variable in the above described scenario and state the statistical name of the other variable. b) Use a statistical calculator to determine … i. … the value of the product moment correlation coefficient between N and T . ( ) 1\" style=\"--madas-width:573.3px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q2-question-2.svg\" alt=\"ii. … the equation of the regression line between T and N , giving the answer in the form T = a + bN , where a and b are constants. ( ) 2 c) Interpret in the context of this question the physical meaning of a and b . ( ) 3 Comment further on the likely value of a in a real life scenario. d) Use the equation of the regression line to estimate the value of T when … i. … N = 1600 . ii. … N = 825 .\" style=\"--madas-width:576.0px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q2-question-3.svg\" alt=\"Comment further on the reliability of each of these estimates. ( ) 4\" style=\"--madas-width:584.0px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q2-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"(a) N is the explanatory variable, as the number of revolutions is used to explain the temperature: T is the response variable: [A1] (b) Enter the five paired observations (600, 49), (700, 47), (900, 52), (1050, 52), (1300, 53) in a two-variable calculator and select linear regression with N first and T second: Then (i) the PMCC is r ≈0.8452 . [A1] (ii) the least-squares line for T on N is ˆT = 43.6875 + 0.00759615N . [A1] (c) The intercept a estimates the temperature at N = 0. [M1] A stationary drill bit would generally be near the ambient temperature, and N = 0 is far outside the observed range: [A1] The slope b estimates an increase of 0.00760 ◦C per extra revolution: [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q2-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"(d) Substitution into the regression line gives (i) at N = 1600, calculate 43.6875 + 0.00759615(1600) = 55.8413 . . ., giving ˆT ≈55.8 ◦C . [A1] The estimate extrapolates beyond 1300, the largest observed N. [A1] (ii) at N = 825, 43.6875 + 0.00759615(825) = 49.9543 . . ., giving ˆT ≈50.0 ◦C . [A1] This is an interpolation within the observed range and is more reliable under the model: [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 2 is 10 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "The table below shows the number of revolutions of a drill bit N , and the maximum temperature T C ° reached by this drill bit after revolving for one minute. N 600 700 900 1050 1300 T 49 47 52 52 53 a) State, with a reason, which is the explanatory variable in the above described scenario and state the statistical name of the other variable. b) Use a statistical calculator to determine … i. … the value of the product moment correlation coefficient between N and T . ( ) 1 ii. … the equation of the regression line between T and N , giving the answer in the form T = a + bN , where a and b are constants. ( ) 2 c) Interpret in the context of this question the physical meaning of a and b . ( ) 3 Comment further on the likely value of a in a real life scenario. d) Use the equation of the regression line to estimate the value of T when … i. … N = 1600 . ii. … N = 825 . Comment further on the reliability of each of these estimates. ( ) 4"
