    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-c/q4",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q4-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The times taken to complete a 3 mile run, in minutes, by the members of a jogging club are summarized in the table below. Times Frequency (nearest hour) 11 – 14 24 15 – 17 24 18 – 19 19 20 11 21 – 23 21 24 – 28 15\" style=\"--madas-width:558.7px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q4-question-2.svg\" alt=\"a) Estimate the mean and standard deviation of this data. Workings must be shown in this part. ( ) 6 b) Estimate, by linear interpolation, the median of this data. ( ) 2 c) Draw an accurate histogram to represent this data. ( ) 4 d) Find the proportion of data which lies within 3 standard deviations of the mean. ( ) 2 e) Discuss briefly whether this data could be modelled by a Normal distribution. ( ) 3\" style=\"--madas-width:569.3px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q4-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Worked solution diagram\" style=\"--madas-width:562.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q4-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Each midpoint is the mean of the class boundaries and each density is frequency divided by class width: Thus the densities are 24/4, 24/3, 19/2, 11/1, 21/3, 15/5. The grouped times give the midpoints and frequency densities below: Time (min) 11–14 15–17 18–19 20 21–23 24–28 Midpoint x 12.5 16 18.5 20 22 26 Frequency f 24 24 19 11 21 15 Class width 4 3 2 1 3 5 Frequency density 6 8 9.5 11 7 3 (a) P f = 114, [B1] [A1] P fx = 2107.5 [A1] and P fx2 = 41100.75. [A1] Then s ¯x = 2107.5 41100.75 2107.5 2 ≈18.49 min , σ = − ≈4.33 min . [M1] [A1] 114 114 114\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q4-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"(b) The cumulative frequencies are 24, 48, 67, 78, 99, 114, so 114/2 = 57 lies in the third class: The median is at cumulative frequency 57. The cumulative frequency just below the 17.5–19.5 class is 48. [B1] Interpolation gives 17.5 + 2(57 −48)/19 ≈18.45 min . [A1] (c) The bar edges are 10.5, 14.5, 17.5, 19.5, 20.5, 23.5, 28.5. Plot class boundaries against frequency density. [M1] Use class boundaries [B1] label and scale the frequency-density axis [B1] plot the calculated bar heights [B1] draw adjoining bars of the correct widths (d) The bounds ¯x ± 3σ are approximately 5.49 and 31.48 minutes: [B1] The source rounds the mean first and obtains 18.5 + 3(4.33) = 31.49 and 18.5 −3(4.33) = 5.51. Both calculations include all six classes: Every class lies within them, so the proportion is 1 . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q4-solution-4.svg\" alt=\"(e) The mean and median are close, and all observations lie within three standard deviations: [B1] The histogram has a tapered right side and some asymmetry: [A1] A normal model is therefore questionable: [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:673.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 4 is 17 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "The times taken to complete a 3 mile run, in minutes, by the members of a jogging club are summarized in the table below. Times Frequency (nearest hour) 11 – 14 24 15 – 17 24 18 – 19 19 20 11 21 – 23 21 24 – 28 15 a) Estimate the mean and standard deviation of this data. Workings must be shown in this part. ( ) 6 b) Estimate, by linear interpolation, the median of this data. ( ) 2 c) Draw an accurate histogram to represent this data. ( ) 4 d) Find the proportion of data which lies within 3 standard deviations of the mean. ( ) 2 e) Discuss briefly whether this data could be modelled by a Normal distribution. ( ) 3"
