    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-c/q5",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q5-question-1.svg\" alt=\"A test statistic has distribution ( ) B 40, p . Given that p = p ≠ H : 0.2 , H : 0.2 , 0 1 find the critical region for the test statistic at the 5% significance level. ( ) 5\" style=\"--madas-width:572.0px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q5-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Let p be the population proportion: Test H0 : p = 0.2 against H1 : p̸ = 0.2. Under H0, X ∼B(40, 0.2). [B1] For a two-tailed test, use the largest rejection region with probability at most 2.5% in each tail: P(X ≤2) ≈0.00794 P(X ≤3) ≈0.02846, [M1] [A1] P(X ≥14) ≈0.01941 P(X ≥13) ≈0.04324. The lower-tail values are 0.794% &lt; 2.5% and 2.846% &gt; 2.5%. For the upper tail, P(X ≥13) = 1 −P(X ≤12) ≈1 −0.95676 = 0.04324 and P(X ≥14) = 1 −P(X ≤13) ≈1 −0.98059 . = 0.01941 Only the latter lies below 2.5%. Hence the critical region is {0, 1, 2} ∪{14, 15, . . . , 40} . [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 5 is 5 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "A test statistic has distribution ( ) B 40, p . Given that p = p ≠ H : 0.2 , H : 0.2 , 0 1 find the critical region for the test statistic at the 5% significance level. ( ) 5"
  },
