    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-c/q6",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q6-question-1.svg\" alt=\"Markus is a health fanatic. On a given day, the probabilities that he goes for a run, he uses the gym or he cycles are 0.5, 0.4 and 0.1, respectively. Markus sometimes uses the sauna after these activities. The probability he uses the sauna after he goes for a run is 0.1 . The respective probabilities for using the sauna after using the gym or cycling are 0.6 and 0.3. Find the probability that on a given day Markus … a) … will use the sauna. ( ) 4\" style=\"--madas-width:574.7px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q6-question-2.svg\" alt=\"b) … used the gym, given he used the sauna. ( ) 2 c) … did not go for a run, given he did not use the sauna. ( ) 3\" style=\"--madas-width:574.7px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q6-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Using a tree diagram: Let S denote sauna use:\" style=\"--madas-width:508.0px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q6-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Multiply the initial branch probability by its conditional sauna or no-sauna probability. Joint with S′ Activity Activity probability Joint with S Run 0.50 0.50(0.10) = 0.05 0.50(0.90) = 0.45 Gym 0.40 0.40(0.60) = 0.24 0.40(0.40) = 0.16 Cycle 0.10 0.10(0.30) = 0.03 0.10(0.70) = 0.07 (a) P(S) = 0.05 + 0.24 + 0.03 = 0.32 . [A1] Run and sauna [A1] gym and sauna [A1] cycling and sauna [A1] sum the three disjoint probabilities (b) P(gym | S) = P(gym ∩S)/ P(S) = 0.24/0.32 . [M1] [A1] = 0.75\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q6-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"(c) The numerator combines the gym and cycling paths without sauna: The denominator includes all three no-sauna paths, 0.45 + 0.16 + 0.07 = 0.68 = 1 −0.32. Thus P(no run | S′) = (0.16 + 0.07)/0.68 . [M1] [M1] [A1] = 23 68 ≈0.3382\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 6 is 9 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "Markus is a health fanatic. On a given day, the probabilities that he goes for a run, he uses the gym or he cycles are 0.5, 0.4 and 0.1, respectively. Markus sometimes uses the sauna after these activities. The probability he uses the sauna after he goes for a run is 0.1 . The respective probabilities for using the sauna after using the gym or cycling are 0.6 and 0.3. Find the probability that on a given day Markus … a) … will use the sauna. ( ) 4 b) … used the gym, given he used the sauna. ( ) 2 c) … did not go for a run, given he did not use the sauna. ( ) 3"
  },
  {
    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-c/q7",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q7-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The contents of bottles of olive oil, in ml, produced by a small factory are Normally distributed with mean µ and standard deviation 5. a) Find the value of µ if 97.5% of these bottles contain more than 994 ml. ( ) 4 b) Using the value of µ found in part (a), determine the probability that a bottle picked at random will contain less than 992 ml. ( ) 4 An inspector visits the factory and selects a bottle at random. If the bottle contains less than 992 ml, the factory will get fined. c) Given that the factory got fined, determine the probability that the bottle that the inspector examined contained less than 990 ml. ( ) 5\" style=\"--madas-width:569.3px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q7-question-2.svg\" alt=\"Question diagram\" style=\"--madas-width:570.7px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q7-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Looking at the diagram below:\" style=\"--madas-width:561.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q7-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Let X ∼N(µ, 52) be the contents of the bottle in millilitres: (a) Since P(X &gt; 994) = 0.975, the area to its left is 1 −0.975 = 0.025. Thus P(X &lt; 994) = 0.025 [B1] gives (994 −µ)/5 = Φ−1(0.025) ≈−1.959964. [A1] Therefore 994 −µ ≈5(−1.96) = −9.8, giving µ = 994 + 9.8. Hence µ ≈1003.8 ml . [M1] [A1] (b) The standardised value is (992 −1003.8)/5 = −2.36. By symmetry the lower tail has area 1 −Φ(2.36) ≈1 −0.9909 = 0.0091. With extra precision, P(X &lt; 992) = Φ((992 −µ)/5) ≈0.009138 . [M1] Use the fitted mean [M1] standardise 992 [M1] choose the lower tail [A1] evaluate its probability\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q7-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"(c) A bottle with less than 990 ml also has less than 992 ml. [B1] [A1] The numerator is P((X &lt; 990) ∩(X &lt; 992)) = P(X &lt; 990). The standardised cutoff is (990 −1003.8)/5 = −2.76, so the lower-tail probability is 1 −Φ(2.76) ≈1 −0.99711 = 0.00289. With the rounded values the source obtains 0.00289/0.0091 = 0.31758 . . . . Use the normal CDF and divide by the probability of a fine: P(X &lt; 990 | X &lt; 992) = Φ((990 −µ)/5) Φ((992 −µ)/5) ≈0.00289039 0.00913835 ≈0.3163 . [M1] [M1] [A1] Source note. The handwritten calculation rounds both tail areas before dividing and obtains 0.318. The value above keeps additional digits.\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 7 is 13 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "The contents of bottles of olive oil, in ml, produced by a small factory are Normally distributed with mean µ and standard deviation 5. a) Find the value of µ if 97.5% of these bottles contain more than 994 ml. ( ) 4 b) Using the value of µ found in part (a), determine the probability that a bottle picked at random will contain less than 992 ml. ( ) 4 An inspector visits the factory and selects a bottle at random. If the bottle contains less than 992 ml, the factory will get fined. c) Given that the factory got fined, determine the probability that the bottle that the inspector examined contained less than 990 ml. ( ) 5 "
  },
