    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q14-question-1.svg\" alt=\"12 N 30° B A box B of mass 1.25 kg is pulled along rough horizontal ground by a force of magnitude 12 N inclined at 30° to the horizontal, as shown in the figure above. The box is modelled as a particle moving on a rough horizontal plane where coefficient of friction between the particle and the plane is 0.25. a) Determine the acceleration of the box. ( ) 6\" style=\"--madas-width:574.7px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q14-question-2.svg\" alt=\"ms−. 1 The pulling force is suddenly removed when the box has a speed of 7.35 b) Find the time it takes the box to come to rest from the instant the pulling force was removed. ( ) 6\" style=\"--madas-width:573.3px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q14-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Starting with a detailed diagram: (a) While pulled, R + 12 sin 30◦= 1.25g [B1] [A1] gives R + 6 = 12.25, hence R = 6.25 N. [A1] The friction is 0.25R = 1.5625 N. [A1] By Newton’s second law along the ground,\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q14-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"√ 1.25a = 12 cos 30◦−1.5625 = 6 a ≈7.06 m s−2 . 3 −1.5625, [M1] [A1] (b) After removal, R′ = 1.25g [B1] and the only horizontal force is friction: −µR′ = 1.25a′, hence −0.25(1.25g) = 1.25a′. Thus friction causes acceleration −0.25g = −2.45 m s−2, [A1] taking forward as positive: [M1] Then 0 = 7.35 −2.45t, [A1] giving 2.45t = 7.35, hence t = 3 s . [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 14 is 12 marks.</p>"
