    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-c/q15",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q15-question-1.svg\" alt=\"P A B 3 kg 4 kg α\" style=\"--madas-width:434.7px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q15-question-2.svg\" alt=\"Two particles A and B , of mass 3 kg and 4 kg respectively, are attached to each of the ends of a light inextensible string. The string passes over a smooth pulley P , at the top of a fixed rough plane, inclined at α to the horizontal, where tan α = 0.75 . Particle A is placed at rest on the incline plane while B is hanging freely at the end of the incline plane vertically below P , as shown in the figure above. The two particles, the pulley and the string lie in a vertical plane parallel to the line of greatest slope of the incline plane. The particles are released from rest with the string taut. Particle A begins to move up the incline plane, where the constant ground friction between A and the plane has magnitude 10.5 N .\" style=\"--madas-width:561.3px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q15-question-3.svg\" alt=\"Ignoring air resistance, calculate ... a) ... the acceleration of the system immediately after the particles are released. ( ) 6 b)  ... the magnitude and direction of the force exerted by the string on P . ( ) 4 2 s after release, while both particles are moving, the string breaks. c) Calculate the total distance A moves up the plane from the instant since the particles were released, assuming that A does not reach the pulley. ( ) 7\" style=\"--madas-width:573.3px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q15-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Start with a detailed diagram:\" style=\"--madas-width:536.0px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q15-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Since tan α = 3/4, sin α = 3/5. Let T be the tension and a the common acceleration magnitude: (a) Resolve for A up the plane and B downwards: T −3g(3/5) −10.5 = 3a, 4g −T = 4a. [M1] [A1] Adding gives 7a = 39.2 −17.64 −10.5 , [M1] [A1] = 11.06 so a = 1.58 m s−2 . [M1] [A1] (b) From 4g −T = 4a, T = 4g −4a = 4(9.8) −4(1.58) = 32.88 N. [M1] The two pulls on the pulley have this same magnitude and meet at 90◦−α = 53.13◦. [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q15-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"The angle to the vertical is (90◦−arctan(3/4))/2 because the forces have equal magnitudes: The resultant bisects their angle. It acts downwards at 26.6◦[A1] to the vertical towards the plane and has magnitude 2(32.88) cos 26.565◦≈58.8 N . [A1] (c) In the first two seconds, A moves 1 2(1.58)(2)2 = 3.16 m [B1] and reaches v = 0 + 1.58(2) = 3.16 m s−1. [A1] The same distance follows from s = (u + v)t/2 = (0 + 3.16)(2)/2 . = 3.16\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-c/q15-solution-4.svg\" alt=\"After the break, both gravity’s component down the plane and friction oppose the uphill motion: [A1] The acceleration up the plane is a′ = −17.64 + 10.5 = −9.38 m s−2. [M1] [A1] 3 Now v2 = u2 + 2a′s with v = 0 gives 0 = 3.162 + 2(−9.38)s, hence 18.76s = 9.9856. The remaining distance is 3.162/(2 · 9.38) ≈0.5323 m. [A1] The total is 3.16 + 0.5323 ≈3.69 m . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:645.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 15 is 17 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "P A B 3 kg 4 kg α Two particles A and B , of mass 3 kg and 4 kg respectively, are attached to each of the ends of a light inextensible string. The string passes over a smooth pulley P , at the top of a fixed rough plane, inclined at α to the horizontal, where tan α = 0.75 . Particle A is placed at rest on the incline plane while B is hanging freely at the end of the incline plane vertically below P , as shown in the figure above. The two particles, the pulley and the string lie in a vertical plane parallel to the line of greatest slope of the incline plane. The particles are released from rest with the string taut. Particle A begins to move up the incline plane, where the constant ground friction between A and the plane has magnitude 10.5 N . Ignoring air resistance, calculate ... a) ... the acceleration of the system immediately after the particles are released. ( ) 6 b)  ... the magnitude and direction of the force exerted by the string on P . ( ) 4 2 s after release, while both particles are moving, the string breaks. c) Calculate the total distance A moves up the plane from the instant since the particles were released, assuming that A does not reach the pulley. ( ) 7"
  }
